xfilter for Debian
==================

Where the files went
--------------------

The program is two Python files that must sit in the same directory, so both
are installed to /usr/share/xfilter and /usr/bin/xfilter is a link to
xfilter.py there.  /usr/bin/xfilter.py is a second link to the same file,
because that is the name the README and the shell helpers use.

The shell helpers
-----------------

xfilter.bash defines `xssh`, which starts a trust domain's filter if it is not
already up and then sshs through it.  It is not sourced for you -- add this to
~/.bashrc (or ~/.zshrc):

    . /usr/share/xfilter/xfilter.bash

What is a Recommends, and why
-----------------------------

openssh-client is a Recommends rather than a Depends.  `--ssh HOST` runs
`ssh -X -o ForwardX11Trusted=yes HOST`, and the shipped `xssh` helper does the
same, so almost every install wants it -- but the proxy is equally a wall
around a *local* application you do not trust (`xfilter -- xterm`,
`xfilter --use NAME -- cmd`), and that use needs no ssh at all.  There is no
version bound because there is nothing recent to require: ForwardX11Trusted
has been an OpenSSH option since 3.8, in 2004.  It is openssh-client by name
rather than the virtual ssh-client, because that option is OpenSSH's, not
every ssh client's.  With ssh missing, `--ssh` fails immediately and says so
("cannot run ssh: ..."), rather than starting a proxy nothing connects to.

The clipboard prompt
--------------------

`--gate ask` is the setting that gives you a working clipboard in both
directions.  It needs python3-gi and gir1.2-gtk-3.0, which are Recommends
rather than Depends: the proxy itself is Python 3 standard library only, and
`--gate deny` (the default) and `--gate allow` do not draw anything.

It also needs somewhere to draw, which no package can supply.  The prompt
opens on your real display (--upstream), deliberately not on the filtered one,
so on a headless machine there is nowhere to put it.

Running one per trust domain under systemd
------------------------------------------

/usr/share/doc/xfilter/examples/xfilter@.service is a user template unit, one
instance per domain.  Copy it to ~/.config/systemd/user/ and start an instance:

    systemctl --user start xfilter@$(systemd-escape work@buildbox)

Everything behind one filter can read everything else behind it, so the
instance name is the security boundary: one per account, or per application
you do not trust.

